==English==
{{was wotd|2018|May|10}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|comminūtiō||breaking into pieces, crumbling, shattering; crushing, pulverizing}}, from {{der|en|la|comminuō||to break or crumble into small pieces; to crush, pulverize}} (from {{m|la|com-||''prefix indicating completeness''}} + {{m|la|minuō||to make smaller; to diminish, lessen}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*mey-||little, small}}) + {{der|en|la|-tiō||''suffix forming a noun relating to some action or the result of an action''}}; equivalent to {{suffix|comminute|ion|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˌkɒmɪˈnjuːʃ(ə)n/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˌkɑməˈn(j)uʃən/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|com|mi|nu|tion|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|often|mining|waste management}} The [[breaking#Noun|breaking]] or [[grinding#Noun|grinding]] up of a material to form smaller [[particle]]s.
#* {{quote-magazine|magazine=Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London|date=March 1695|passage={{...}} the two first still applying the less divided parts of the Aliment, to the ''Dentes Molares'', till there's an equal '''Comminution''' of all its Parts {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Physico-theology; Or, A Demonstration of the Being and Attributes of God, from His Works of Creation|author=William Derham|year=1742|passage=It's hooked Form is of great Use to the rapacious Kind, in catching and holding their Prey, and in the '''Comminution''' thereof by tearing; to others it is no less serviceable to their Climbing, as well as neat and nice '''Comminution''' of their Food.}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Essentials of Practice of Pharmacy|page=35|author=Lucius Elmer Sayre|year=1894|passage=What circumstances determine the degree of '''comminution''' to which a substance should be subjected preparatory to future extraction?}}
#* {{quote-book|author=J. P. La Fage|author2=W. L. Nutting|chapter=Nutrient Dynamics of Termites|editor=M. V. Brian|title=Production Ecology of Ants and Termites|series=International Biological Programme|seriesvolume=13|location=Cambridge; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|year=1978|page=165|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4EA9AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA165|isbn=978-0-521-21519-0|passage=Termites are herbivores and detritivores variously involved in the '''comminution''' and decomposition of vegetable matter, through most of the warm temperate and tropical zones.}}
# {{lb|en|[[traumatology]]}} The [[fracture#Noun|fracture]] of a [[bone#Noun|bone]] [[site#Noun|site]] in [[multiple]] [[piece#Noun|pieces]] (technically, at least three); [[crumbling#Noun|crumbling]].
#* {{quote-book|title=On the fractures of bones occurring in gun-shot injuries|author=George Friedrich Louis Stromeyer|year=1856|passage='''Comminution''' of the shaft of one or of both bones of the fore-arm does not of itself indicate operative interference, other complications may necessitate primary or secondary amputation.}}

====Usage notes====
In [[traumatology]], comminution is to be distinguished from a [[compound#Adjective|compound]] or [[open]] [[fracture#Noun|fracture]], though fractures which are both comminuted and compound do occur.

